#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065076} :activity/id #uuid "0dc1fb7b-6336-57cd-b507-8bd6ddfc25fc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048189 :topic/id #uuid "86b8f7ea-5908-5137-a377-052e6fc4f731" :topic/math-academy-id 315} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065433 :tutorial/content "Consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral, apart from the coefficient of $4$ in the denominator, resembles the basic integral\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nIn fact, they look almost the same, but instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $4x^2 = (2x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can often use this trick whenever we see an integral that closely resembles an integral for an inverse trigonometric function.\n\n**Note:** As always, after solving an integral using substitution, we should always double-check that our result is correct. If we differentiate the result, then it should come out to the original integrand:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)+C] & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)]+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "53af5e98-4580-5243-a97c-de9e0d5f8fab" :tutorial/math-academy-id 863 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d3e15f90-13b8-5437-b44e-fcdec07ad48c" :step/math-academy-id 641164 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064218 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064090 :question/id #uuid "48ec6930-88b7-5aad-9bb7-c5e23e766f80" :question/math-academy-id "e-470"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de1e3f3a-5a64-573f-8fb1-9952fa36a839" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050628} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050675}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250163} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250165} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290599} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342064}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Sine"} :step/id #uuid "c5880b8e-1ebf-531e-9458-f3312a599e31" :step/math-academy-id 641165 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064219 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064091 :question/id #uuid "3e89a278-54ab-59a6-adb6-38f43c0fbe9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-471"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "227abe29-6e31-5afd-b0ca-3b8ed7d8c287" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250171} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332738} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332739} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428006}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "a6a53452-9624-525f-9b54-d025309e71da" :step/math-academy-id 641168 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065124}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064220 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064092 :question/id #uuid "cb771ff3-b5a9-5900-913f-df23957760b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-472"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2f9806d-3a72-5f0a-a6f4-db8dad8871f6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048332}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250186} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287945} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319105} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428007}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Definite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Trigonometric Functions"} :step/id #uuid "e5a617a2-9f5d-550f-a473-195c08b4798c" :step/math-academy-id 641174 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065182}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064250 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064101 :question/id #uuid "56860611-869d-5035-bf8f-f568c774d78d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5014"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d18bdc7-9387-5b2d-98bd-015fa4a1eba7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250174} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332740} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335977} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342066} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342068}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Indefinite Integrals Using Substitution with Inverse Secant"} :step/id #uuid "1c6cd5c4-2775-585d-9234-d995757ade98" :step/math-academy-id 641171 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065182 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065381 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we learned how to use substitution to evaluate integrals that give rise to inverse trigonometric functions.\n\nAlthough you can always solve these integrals by going through the substitution process, it is convenient to remember the following key results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(ax)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{a}^{2}-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{x}{a})+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+(ax)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(ax)+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{a}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{a})+C \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{|ax|\\sqrt{(ax)^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{a}\\text{arcsec}(ax)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remember the results above, note the following:\n\n- Whenever the integrand has an $ax$ term, the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $ax.$\n\n- Otherwise, if the $a$ and $x$ are separated, then the argument of the resulting inverse trigonometric function is $\\dfrac{x}{a}.$\n\n- All the results are multiplied by $\\dfrac{1}{a},$ except for $\\arcsin \\left( \\dfrac{x}{a} \\right) + C.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "02539765-4345-5409-87f9-9901a59b7ebf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4800 :tutorial/title "Summary of Key Results"} :step/id #uuid "b420ebdd-a5d6-5ce1-8cde-23990b2968f7" :step/math-academy-id 641177}] :activity/title "Integration by Substitution With Inverse Trigonometric Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064411}]}
